  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 316 PM EST Sat Nov 7 2026\n\nPattern Synopsis/Forecast: Pacific-influenced flow pattern continues \nacross North America with cold air bottled up above the Arctic \nCircle.  Split flow across the CONUS emanating around a strong upper \nlow over the northeast Pacific; flow splits over the nation's \nmidsection with several waves of note in the northern branch.  Upper \nlow continues to spin across the Great Lakes today (dynamic \ntropopause down to 650mb over western New York/Pennsylvania)...with \nupstream short wave troughs over northwest Ontario/upper Great \nLakes/Midwest...and another coming out of the Canadian Rockies. \nInterestingly...the trough moving into the Great Lakes is \naccompanied by short wave ridging at 700mb/850mb...along with drier \nair moving into Michigan as flow transitions from cyclonic to \nanticyclonic.  This trough over ridge juxtaposition also resulting \nin steep mid level lapse rates beneath the thermal trough aloft. \nLingering cyclonic flow in the boundary layer today across Lower \nMichigan...with a narrow ridge of high pressure extending from he \nMidwest northeast across Wisconsin/Upper Michigan into northeast \nOntario/northern Quebec.   \n   \nShort wave trough coming out of the Canadian Rockies will be the \nnext feature of interest as it digs southeast across the Great Lakes \nSunday.  Associated surface low remains north of Lake Superior but a \ncold/occluded front will cross Michigan Sunday.  This will be \nfollowed by another low-amplitude ripple Monday night/Tuesday before \nthe pattern starts to amplify for midweek.  Strong Pacific jet \ngooses the eastern Pacific trough with resultant downstream energy \npropagation.  Long wave trough gets carved out across eastern North \nAmerica for the latter half of the week...evolution of this trough \nand its various components will play a big role in surface evolution \nand potential cyclogenesis in the Wednesday-Thursday time frame.  At \nthe very least a nice push of colder air into the Great Lakes is in \nthe offing...along with some precipitation type issues.\n\nPrimary Forecast Concerns:  \n\nSome rain chances return Sunday along with breezy/windy conditions: \nLittle bowling ball of upper level energy will roll across the\nLake Superior basin Sunday...most of the deep moisture with this\nfeature seems to lag the better dynamics. It may be that best\nrain chances will be with lake effect/lake enhancement Sunday\nafternoon/Sunday night as boundary layer flow comes back around\nto the northwest and 850mb temperatures sink below zero.\nBoundary layer winds will begin to increase again tonight...some\ngustiness due to mechanical turbulence but stronger winds\nexpected Sunday with onset of cold advection and mixing/\ndownward momentum transfer. Wind gusts 25-35mph expected...\n30-40mph around Whitefish Bay Sunday afternoon/evening (Gale\nWatch up for Whitefish Bay). Winds will gradually diminish\nSunday night/Monday morning as a surface ridge builds in from \nthe west. \n\nMidweek precipitation chances??: Cold air does look to spill across \nthe Great Lakes starting Wednesday...so there will be an increase in \nlake convection as 850mb temperatures drop at or below -8C toward \nThursday.  How much of a synoptic component to precipitation chances \nis a question for Wednesday...dependent upon low level cyclogenesis. \nOverall PoPs for midweek are low given the uncertainty regarding \nsynoptic potential...and most of the precipitation probabilities for \nThursday are hugging the Lake Michigan/Lake Huron shorelines with a \nnortherly component to the boundary layer flow (for now).",
